     "text": "Moore Vasquez and Jefferson Medical Group\nRe: Stephanie Perkins (DOB 10/21/1959)\n\nDr. Jeffrey Meyer\nMoore Vasquez and Jefferson Medical Group\nMay 22, 2024\n\nTo Whom It May Concern,\n\nI am writing to provide a detailed medical summary for Ms. Stephanie Perkins, who was last seen in our practice on May 22, 2024, regarding her chronic kidney disease with an estimated glomerular filtration rate (eGFR). Ms. Perkins is a 64-year-old female who has been under our care since the onset of her kidney disease in September 2019.\n\nDuring today's visit, Ms. Perkins reported feeling well overall. Her blood pressure was recorded at 128/90 mmHg, and her weight was 158 pounds. These values are within acceptable ranges given her condition. She has been managing her chronic kidney disease with ongoing dialysis and has had a kidney transplant for the past 2.3 years. There have been no or issues with the transplant to date.\n\nHer blood pressure and weight have been relatively stable over the past few visits. For reference, on January 12, 2024, her blood pressure was 132/87 mmHg and her weight was 148 pounds. On September 2, 2023, her blood pressure was 126/81 mmHg and her weight was 156 pounds. On April 24, 2023, her blood pressure was 124/85 mmHg and her weight was 153 pounds. On December 13, 2022, her blood pressure was 132/85 mmHg and her weight was 151 pounds. On August 3, 2022, her blood pressure was mmHg and her weight was 161 pounds. On March 25, 2022, her blood pressure was 134/90 mmHg and her weight was 156 pounds. On November 13, 2021, her blood pressure was 131/80 mmHg and her weight was 158 pounds. On July 5, 2021, her blood pressure 121/85 mmHg her weight was 160 pounds.\n\nMs. Perkins' kidney function has been closely monitored, and her eGFR has remained stable, indicating effective management of her condition. She has been compliant with her treatment regimen, which includes regular dialysis sessions and immunosuppressive medications post-transplant.\n\nSocially, Ms. Perkins has made positive lifestyle changes. She was a former cigarette smoker, consuming about 1.5 packs per day, but she quit smoking in April 2024. She has been honest and forthcoming about her smoking history. Regarding alcohol consumption, Ms. Perkins reports consuming approximately 12 drinks per week, which is within moderate limits.\n\nIn summary, Ms. Perkins is doing well with her chronic kidney disease. Her condition is managed effectively with ongoing dialysis and a successful kidney transplant. She maintains a stable weight and blood pressure, and her kidney function"
